---
// The styles of the game belong to the page: the picture of the game that the page renders on the
// server and the game itself use the same ones, which are sent once
import './components/root-cause.css';

import { type GameDefinition } from '@/games/registry';
import GameLayout from '@/layouts/GameLayout.astro';

import RootCause from './components/RootCause';
import RootCauseSkeleton from './components/RootCauseSkeleton';

interface Props {
  game: GameDefinition;
}

const { game } = Astro.props;
---

<GameLayout game={game}>
  {/* Rendered in the browser only: nothing of the game is worth sending as HTML first */}
  <RootCause client:only="react">
    <RootCauseSkeleton slot="fallback" />
  </RootCause>
  <noscript>
    <p class="text-muted">
      This game needs JavaScript to run. Nothing is sent anywhere, it all happens in your browser.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div slot="rules">
    <p>
      Every level is a map divided into rooms. As many people (in <strong>Outage</strong>: devices)
      stand on it as the map has rows, and{' '}
      <strong>exactly one stands in every row and in every column</strong>. All but one are
      suspects, the last one is the <strong>victim</strong>. Your job is to put everybody where they
      belong.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The rule behind every level:</strong> the victim shares a room with exactly one
        suspect. That suspect is the culprit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The clues</strong> tell you where the suspects are. The victim never gets a clue and
        is never named. Some clues are about the whole map and count everybody, the victim too.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Walls</strong> are the edges of the map and the thick lines between rooms.{' '}
        <strong>Next to</strong> means the four cells straight above, below, left or right, inside
        the same room.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Objects:</strong> the outlined ones (a chair to sit on, a rack to mount a device in)
        can be used, the solid ones block their cell. A sofa has its back to a wall. A sofa or a
        cable tray is one object over two cells: you are next to it when you stand beside it, not
        when you sit on it. A bright bar on a wall is a window: it belongs to the cells on both
        sides of the wall, so "in front of a window" and "a room with a window" count for both
        rooms. The <strong>Legend</strong> button under the map explains every picture, and the line
        under the map says what is on the cell you point at.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Two looks:</strong> <em>Case file</em> is about detectives and suspects in a tech
        company, <em>Outage</em> about a network where the failed device shared a room with the
        device that caused it. The rules are the same.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Choose a person, then click a cell to place them. Switch to <strong>Note</strong> to mark
      cells where the chosen person could be, and to <strong>Cross</strong> to rule out cells where
      nobody is. A person on the map crosses out their whole row and column by themselves (nobody
      else can be placed on those crosses), hides the notes about themselves, and everything comes
      back when they are taken off. A clue is ticked while the people on the map satisfy it, and
      turns red while they break it. There is no other feedback while you play. A pencil after a
      name means that the person has notes on the map. Stuck? The <strong>Hint</strong> button
      points at the next person to look at in three steps, from a push to the cell itself, and
      counts the hints you used. At the last step <em>Why here?</em> explains the reasoning. When
      everybody is in the right place, the level is solved and the culprit is shown.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div slot="keys">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <kbd>1</kbd> to <kbd>9</kbd> choose a person
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>P</kbd>, <kbd>N</kbd>, <kbd>X</kbd> switch between place, note and cross
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>H</kbd> asks for a hint (press again for the next stage)
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Arrow keys</kbd> move over the map, <kbd>Enter</kbd> or <kbd>Space</kbd> use the
        current mode on the cell
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Backspace</kbd> clears a cell, <kbd>Esc</kbd> lets go of the chosen person
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Z</kbd> undoes, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Y</kbd> redoes
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div slot="built">
    <p>
      The rules, the clues and the solvers are plain TypeScript with unit tests. Two solvers that
      share no search code (one searches, one reasons the way a person does) must find exactly the
      stored solution of every level, and the second one also rates how hard a level is. The
      interface only draws what the rules say and keeps your moves; nothing is stored on your
      device.
    </p>
  </div>
</GameLayout>
